  "question": {
    "text": "A travelling nucleus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;having a de-Broglie wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;spontaneously splits into two nuclei <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math>&nbsp;of equal masses. After the split,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> travels in the same direction while&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> travels in the opposite direction with a speed equal to half of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math>. The values of the de-Broglie wavelengths&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;will be"
  },
